{"name":"Ice Plant","hex":"#cf7ead","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ice plant","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A succulent annual, Mesembryanthemum crystallinum, of the fig marigold family, having pale pink flowers and fleshy leaves covered with glistening water vesicles.","Any of several plants in fig marigold family, Aizoaceae, which were formerly classified in the genus Mesembryanthemum along with Mesembryanthemum crystallinum.\nEspecially, Carpobrotus edulis."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ice_plant"}],"entries":[{"title":"Aizoaceae","blocks":[{"text":"The Aizoaceae, or fig-marigold family, are a large family of dicotyledonous flowering plants containing 135 genera and about 1,800 species. Several genera are commonly named ice plants or carpet weeds. The Aizoaceae are also referred to as vygies in South Africa. Some of the unusual Southern African genera—such as Conophytum, Lithops, Titanopsis, and Pleiospilos —resemble gemstones, rocks, or pebbles, and are sometimes called living stones or mesembs."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Mesembryanthemum_crystallinum.JPG/330px-Mesembryanthemum_crystallinum.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aizoaceae","wikidata":"Q156219"},{"title":"Carpobrotus edulis","blocks":[{"text":"Carpobrotus edulis is a creeping plant with succulent leaves in the fig-marigold family Aizoaceae, native to South Africa. Its common names include hottentot-fig, sour fig, ice plant, highway ice plant, or vygie."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Hottentot_Fig_-_Flowering.jpg/330px-Hottentot_Fig_-_Flowering.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carpobrotus_edulis","wikidata":"Q1368577"},{"title":"Ice","blocks":[{"text":"Ice is water that is frozen into a solid state, typically forming at or below temperatures of 0 °C, 32 °F, or 273.15 K. It occurs naturally on Earth, on other planets, in Oort cloud objects, and as interstellar ice. As a naturally occurring crystalline inorganic solid with an ordered structure, ice is considered to be a mineral. Depending on the presence of impurities such as particles of soil or bubbles of air, it can appear transparent or a more or less opaque bluish-white color."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Ice_Block%2C_Canal_Park%2C_Duluth_%2832752478892%29.jpg/330px-Ice_Block%2C_Canal_Park%2C_Duluth_%2832752478892%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ice","wikidata":"Q23392"},{"title":"Plant","blocks":[{"text":"Plants are the eukaryotic organisms that constitute the kingdom Plantae. They are predominantly photosynthetic or came from photosynthetic ancestors, meaning that they obtain their energy from sunlight by using the green pigment chlorophyll in their chloroplasts to produce sugars from carbon dioxide and water. Over the course of time, some plants have evolved to be parasitic, meaning they take energy from other organisms. Except for glaucophytes, some green algae and a few red algae, most plants are multicellular."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Fr%C3%BChling_bl%C3%BChender_Kirschenbaum.jpg/330px-Fr%C3%BChling_bl%C3%BChender_Kirschenbaum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plant","wikidata":"Q756"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Mesembryanthemum_guerichianum_seedling_IMG_8167-cropped.jpg/500px-Mesembryanthemum_guerichianum_seedling_IMG_8167-cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Mesembryanthemum_guerichianum_seedling_IMG_8167-cropped.jpg/1280px-Mesembryanthemum_guerichianum_seedling_IMG_8167-cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Mesembryanthemum_guerichianum_seedling_IMG_8167-cropped.jpg/500px-Mesembryanthemum_guerichianum_seedling_IMG_8167-cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Mesembryanthemum guerichianum seedling, showing the epidermal bladder cells that inspired the name \"ice plant\"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMesembryanthemum_guerichianum_seedling_IMG_8167-cropped.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Aptenia_cordifolia_flower_leaves.jpg/500px-Aptenia_cordifolia_flower_leaves.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Aptenia_cordifolia_flower_leaves.jpg/500px-Aptenia_cordifolia_flower_leaves.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Mesembryanthemum cordifolium or rock rose","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAptenia_cordifolia_flower_leaves.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Carpobrotus_edulis_4.JPG/500px-Carpobrotus_edulis_4.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Carpobrotus_edulis_4.JPG/1280px-Carpobrotus_edulis_4.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Carpobrotus_edulis_4.JPG/500px-Carpobrotus_edulis_4.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Carpobrotus edulis, an \"ice plant\"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACarpobrotus_edulis_4.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Cheiridopsis_denticulata.jpg/500px-Cheiri